answer.answer:
According to KEP-3331, structured config for OIDC authentication had been requested in various contexts over several years. The flag-based configuration kept growing without bounds, and SIG Auth wanted to replace it with a proper versioned config format. That format would make it easier to support many requested features, including:
- multiple client IDs;
- identifying users by more than one JWT claim;
- mapping any ID token claim into user info extra attributes;
- failing authentication unless the user is in a specific set of groups;
- array support in required claims;
- forwarding audiences to authorization.

The KEP's goals list the customizations the flags couldn't provide:
- richer claim validation rules (the flags only validated the audience, and only by exact value);
- flexible claim mappings, including nested claims (the flags could only pick a single value from a single claim and prefix groups);
- more than one OIDC provider without an external multiplexing provider;
- changing authenticator settings without restarting kube-apiserver;
- validation rules on the final user info object, so infra providers can safely expose this to customers;
- easy migration from the existing flags.

The 1.30 beta blog says SIG Auth recommends the file because it is more flexible and extensible. The `--oidc-*` flags still work, but they can't be combined with `--authentication-config`; doing so is a misconfiguration and the API server reports an error and exits.
